lesson:
# Linked Lists

A linked list is a chain of **nodes**, each holding a value and a reference to the **next** node. You reach any node only by walking from the head — there is no indexing.

```java
class ListNode { int val; ListNode next; ListNode(int v){ val = v; } }

// Build behind a DUMMY head so appends need no special case:
ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0), tail = dummy;
for (int x : values) { tail.next = new ListNode(x); tail = tail.next; }
ListNode head = dummy.next;
```

## When to reach for this
Signals: *'reverse the list'*, *'remove the nth node'*, *'merge sorted lists'*, *'detect a cycle'*, O(1) insert/delete.

## The two survival rules
1. **Cache `next` before you overwrite it** — otherwise the rest of the list is lost.
2. **Use a dummy head** whenever the real head might be removed or reassigned, so you never special-case it.

