lesson:
# Pruning

A correct backtracker can still time out. **Pruning** cuts branches that can't yield a solution.

```java
Arrays.sort(a);
void go(int start, int remaining) {
    if (remaining == 0) { count++; return; }
    for (int i = start; i < a.length; i++) {
        if (a[i] > remaining) break;      // sorted → nothing later fits
        if (i > start && a[i] == a[i-1]) continue; // skip duplicate choice
        go(i, remaining - a[i]);
    }
}
```

## Three standard cuts
| Cut | Why it's safe |
|---|---|
| sort + `break` when candidate > remaining | everything after is even bigger |
| skip `a[i]==a[i-1]` at the same depth | avoids generating a duplicate result |
| bound check before recursing | prune impossible sub-trees early |

