lesson:
# Bellman-Ford

When edges can be negative, or you must cap the number of hops, relax **all edges** repeatedly.

```java
int[] dist = new int[n]; Arrays.fill(dist, INF); dist[src] = 0;
for (int round = 0; round <= k; round++) {   // k+1 rounds = at most k stops
    int[] nd = dist.clone();                  // snapshot: exactly one more edge
    for (int[] e : edges)                      // e = {u, v, w}
        if (dist[e[0]] + e[2] < nd[e[1]]) nd[e[1]] = dist[e[0]] + e[2];
    dist = nd;
}
```

## When to reach for this
Signals: *'at most k stops'*, *'negative weights'*, *'detect a negative cycle'*. The `clone()` snapshot is what enforces the exact hop limit — without it, one round could chain many edges.

# Cycle Detection & Topological Sort

In a **directed** graph, color nodes as you DFS. An edge into a node still on the recursion stack is a **back edge** — a cycle.

```java
int[] color = new int[n];  // 0 white, 1 gray (on stack), 2 black
boolean dfs(int x) {
    color[x] = 1;
    for (int y : adj[x]) {
        if (color[y] == 1) return true;         // back edge → cycle
        if (color[y] == 0 && dfs(y)) return true;
    }
    color[x] = 2; return false;
}
```

## Kahn's topological sort (the acyclic case)
Repeatedly remove indegree-0 nodes; if you emit all n, the graph is acyclic and you have a valid order (course schedule, build order, alien dictionary). Fewer than n → a cycle.

## When to reach for this
Signals: *'can you finish / order the tasks'*, *'prerequisites'*, *'build order'*, *'detect a cycle'*. Undirected graphs use Union-Find or parent-aware DFS instead.

